---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
// Intro bullets, status banner, and why-cards all derive from the repo-root
// README at build time (between marker comments) — see lib/readme.ts.
// Experiment cards derive from ../experiments/*.md — see lib/experiments.ts.
import { readmeSection, readmeWhy } from '../lib/readme';
import { getDesigns } from '../lib/experiments';
import { getPosts } from '../lib/blog';

const github = 'https://github.com/tokedo/KamiBench';

// Copy on this page derives from README.md — reuse, condense, lightly rephrase;
// never escalate claims. The landing is deliberately bullet-dense: the crafted
// prose lives in the blog posts (blog-first restructure, 2026-08-14).

const ideaHtml = readmeSection('IDEA');
const statusHtml = readmeSection('STATUS', { inline: true });
const why = readmeWhy(); // cards only — the WHY block carries no lead paragraph
const designs = getDesigns();
const clPost = getPosts().find((p) => p.slug === 'why-kamibench-for-continual-learning');
const clPostHref = clPost ? `/blog/${clPost.slug}` : '/blog';
---

<Base
  title="KamiBench — an agent benchmark in a persistent world with a verifiable history"
  description="A research program: testing agent continual learning in a persistent world with a verifiable history."
  path="/"
>
  <header class="hero container">
    <h1>KamiBench</h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">
      A research program: testing agent continual learning in a persistent world
      with a verifiable history
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/experiments">Experiments</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/blog">Blog</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/stack">Stack</a>
      <a class="btn" href={github}>GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="https://docs.asphodel.io/kamigotchi">Kamigotchi</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container">
    <aside class="status-banner" role="note">
      <strong>Open research in progress</strong>
      <Fragment set:html={statusHtml} />
    </aside>
  </div>

  <section class="section container" aria-labelledby="idea">
    <h2 id="idea">The idea</h2>
    <div class="section-lead">
      <Fragment set:html={ideaHtml} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container" aria-labelledby="stack">
    <h2 id="stack">One stack, swappable agents, clear metric</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      Different agent configurations are tested against the identical stack: the
      world, the environment interface, and the meter stay fixed while agents
      swap in. The meter prices every run — inference, gas, earnings — so
      performance compares on one traceable number. <a href="/stack">The Stack page</a>
      is everything you need to plug in an agent of your own.
    </p>
    <figure class="stack-figure" aria-label="Stack schematic: swappable agents connect through a fixed environment interface to the world; the meter observes both sides and produces one number, solvency.">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 292" role="img">
        <defs>
          <marker id="lf-head" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="3.5" refY="3.5" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path class="sf-arrow" d="M0.5,0.5 L6.5,3.5 L0.5,6.5 Z"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <!-- swappable agent: two ghost outlines behind the front box -->
        <rect class="sf-ghost" x="46" y="44" width="170" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
        <rect class="sf-ghost" x="38" y="52" width="170" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
        <rect class="sf-box sf-box-agent" x="30" y="60" width="170" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
        <text class="sf-title" x="115" y="92" text-anchor="middle">Agent</text>
        <text class="sf-sub" x="115" y="112" text-anchor="middle">swappable</text>

        <rect class="sf-box" x="275" y="60" width="170" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
        <text class="sf-title" x="360" y="92" text-anchor="middle">Environment interface</text>
        <text class="sf-sub" x="360" y="112" text-anchor="middle">fixed, version-pinned</text>

        <rect class="sf-box" x="520" y="60" width="170" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
        <text class="sf-title" x="605" y="92" text-anchor="middle">The world</text>
        <text class="sf-sub" x="605" y="112" text-anchor="middle">Kamigotchi — live, on-chain</text>

        <!-- agent ⇄ interface ⇄ world -->
        <line class="sf-link" x1="203" y1="96" x2="272" y2="96" marker-start="url(#lf-head)" marker-end="url(#lf-head)"></line>
        <line class="sf-link" x1="448" y1="96" x2="517" y2="96" marker-start="url(#lf-head)" marker-end="url(#lf-head)"></line>

        <!-- meter observes both sides, outputs one number -->
        <rect class="sf-box sf-meter" x="275" y="196" width="170" height="64" rx="6"></rect>
        <text class="sf-title" x="360" y="224" text-anchor="middle">Meter</text>
        <text class="sf-sub" x="360" y="244" text-anchor="middle">inference · gas · earnings</text>
        <path class="sf-tap" d="M115 132 V228 H272" marker-end="url(#lf-head)"></path>
        <path class="sf-tap" d="M605 132 V228 H448" marker-end="url(#lf-head)"></path>
        <text class="sf-out" x="360" y="282" text-anchor="middle">→ profit &amp; loss curves</text>
      </svg>
    </figure>
    <p class="section-lead stack-metric">
      The agent's economy is the metric. The trajectory of its profit-and-loss
      curve — earnings against inference, gas, and infrastructure — shows whether
      the agent is learning: an agent that learns plays better and cheaper over
      time, and its curve bends up; an agent that doesn't stays flat or sinks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why a chain — and why this world</h2>
    <ul class="cards">
      {
        why.cards.map((card) => (
          <li class="card">
            <h3>{card.title}</h3>
            <p set:html={card.html} />
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <div class="why-coda">
      <p>
        The program is portable: Kamigotchi is the best-fit instance we know of today,
        and if a more autonomous world emerges, the same experiments transfer. The honest
        present-tense claim is <strong>tamper-evident, not tamper-proof</strong> — what
        holds today vs. what depends on future governance is stated plainly in the full
        argument. <a href={clPostHref}>Why KamiBench for continual learning →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container" aria-labelledby="experiments">
    <h2 id="experiments">Experiments</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      The registry of controlled experiments, grouped by design: a design fixes the
      protocol — the question, the architecture, the measurement — and each run
      executes it with a pinned manifest of models and stack versions. Designs are
      published and git-timestamped before their first run; results are added as
      runs complete.
    </p>
    <ul class="cards exp-list">
      {
        designs.map((design) => (
          <li class="card exp-card">
            <p class="label">Design</p>
            <h3>
              <a href={`/experiments/${design.slug}`}>{design.title}</a>
            </h3>
            <p>{design.oneliner}</p>
            {design.status && (
              <p class="exp-status">
                <span class="chip chip-pending">Pending</span>
                <span class="exp-status-note">{design.status}</span>
              </p>
            )}
            <ul class="run-list">
              {design.runs.map((run) => (
                <li>
                  <span class={`chip ${run.statusChip}`}>
                    {run.statusLabel}
                  </span>
                  <a class="run-title" href={`/experiments/${run.slug}`}>
                    Run {run.runNumber} — {run.title}
                  </a>
                  <span class="run-meta">Experiment {run.number}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="read-more"><a href="/experiments">All experiments →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
